1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Your email says it uses TLS.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,000
But is the cryptography actually secure?

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
SecureMailScope — evidence-backed cryptographic investigation for email.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,000
An organisation can tell you which mail servers it runs. It usually can't tell
you which TLS versions those servers negotiated last week.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Which cipher suites they accepted, or which sessions had no forward secrecy.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:45,000
An active scanner reports what a server does for a scanner today — not what it
did for real clients during the period you're investigating.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
SecureMailScope reads the packet captures you already have.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
No live probing. No cloud upload. No host in a capture is ever contacted.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
This is a synthetic capture of an IMAP session over TLS.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The capture is reconstructed into sessions, the email protocol is identified
from the dialogue rather than the port, and the TLS handshake is parsed.

11
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Fifty-nine out of a hundred. Weak. Four findings, two of them high priority.

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Notice what the interface leads with: not the score — the things that need
attention.

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Static RSA key exchange. No forward secrecy.

14
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Instead of just saying "weak cryptography", SecureMailScope shows why it was
flagged — the RFC it applies, and what to change.

15
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Packets four and five. You can open the same capture in Wireshark and check.

16
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Here's a TLS 1.3 session. The certificate reads: not available.

17
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:30,000
TLS 1.3 encrypts the certificate message. A passive capture without decryption
material cannot expose what isn't on the wire — so the tool says so.

18
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Two captures of the same server, an hour apart. Same client offer both times.

19
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The server selected TLS 1.2, then TLS 1.0. Because the offer didn't change,
the difference is attributable to the server.

20
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Every investigation exports as JSON, offline HTML, or a forensic PDF.

21
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:22,000
SecureMailScope turns captured email traffic into explainable cryptographic
evidence — so a security team can see what changed, and know what to fix.

22
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
SecureMailScope · SIH26159 · Zero-Day
